<!--
  THE CATALOGUE

  What a settlement can be asked to raise, and what raising it is worth.

  Every entry answers six questions, and the last one is the one that matters:

    Plot       How much ground it stands on. S / M / L / XL, with Open="yes" for a plot with no
               roof and Sky="yes" for one that needs weather and so refuses to go underground.
               The settlement lays a small plot from its first night, a middling one once it is
               a steading, a large one at town, and a grand one only as a city - so a design's
               size is a gate in its own right, on top of MinStage. A design that declares NO
               Plot is a single-cell work raised on one cell: that is what every wall, tower,
               and gatehouse in here is, because a wall is drawn along the frontier and never
               boxed.
    Cost       Drams of water out of the settlement's own casks. Water is what everything here
               is ultimately paid in.
    Materials  What clearing the ground gave back, spent again. mud / canvas / timber / stone /
               marble / scrap. Materials are never minted: they come off a cleared plot, out of
               a caravan, or off the back of something salvaged. The marble house needs marble,
               and marble comes from a seam somebody found.
    Refines    What a processing work makes: shapedtimber / shapedstone / workedmetal (or the
               yard's own key: sawyer / mason / smelter). Declaring it is the whole of what makes
               a building a yard.
    Bits       What a high-craft design costs in the game's own tinkering bits, written in bit
               tiers - Bits="0034" is two of the commonest and one each of tier three and four.
               Drawn from the same dedicated stockpiles as everything else.
    Exotics    Rare finds a great work is finished in: ingot / silver / gold / gem, as
               exotic:units. Nobody here can make one; somebody has to carry it home.
    Staff      Settlers who stand in it and work it. Buildings are people.
    Contents   Population table the finished interior is furnished from, the way a vanilla hut
               is populated. This is where the cask rack, the bunk, the torchpost, and the
               hookah went: a founder orders a house and gets the bunk, rather than ordering
               the bunk. An open plot has no interior and names none.
    Carries    WHAT THIS ADDS TO THE SUSTAINABLE LEVEL, in settlers.

  That last one is the whole denomination of this file. A building's number is not output per
  day; it is how many more people this place honestly carries once nobody is hauling anything
  in. water, food, and roof BIND - the level is the least of the three, because a town with
  water for ninety and bread for nine is a town of nine. craft, spirit, learning, order, and
  luxury LIFT - they are summed and then capped at half the binding level, so no quantity of
  shrines ever outruns the cistern. One point of water is one dram a day sustained, which is
  one settler's thirst at camp rates.

  Improvements climb within a plot; sizes compete across plots. Nothing here grows from a small
  plot into a middling one: to trade up in size you strike what stands there, take the salvage,
  and build again on cleared ground. That is why the housing ladder is tent, then hut, then
  house, then the fine house - four separate designs, each with its own tiers, and not one
  design that metamorphoses.

  Removed from this file and not by accident: the solar condenser. Water that arrives with no
  crew and no fuel is the far end of the autonomy axis, and the far end of that axis is always
  recovered and certified, never commissioned. The condenser is a machine you drag home from a
  ruin and have the keepers pass fit for the grid. Dry ground's early answers are the salt-pan,
  patient collection, and trade - and they are all in here.

  Extending this: ship your own KingdomBuildings.xml from your own mod. Re-using a Key
  overrides that entry entirely, skins and all, so retheming the base catalogue is a supported
  thing to do. See MODDING.md.
-->
